# Knowing which project this is

A note names an element, on a route, in a project — and everything an agent does with it afterwards rests on the project being the one the reviewer meant. A name is typed, so it can be typed at the wrong moment. A directory is where a command happened to run. An address is where something happened to be listening. None of the three can say, on its own, whether two runs of Design mean the same project.

So a project carries an identity of its own: a value generated once, written into the project, and never changed again. Design compares the identity it finds on disk against the one the store recorded, and only then does it agree that the project it is being pointed at is the project whose notes it is about to add to.

That comparison is what makes a moved project ordinary and a mistyped one refused. A repository cloned to another machine, renamed, or served on a different port is the same project with the same identity, and its directory and address are updated without ceremony. A name aimed at a different tree is a different identity, and Design stops rather than quietly rebinding a name that other notes already depend on.

## The file a project keeps

The identity lives beside the project's other Loom settings, in a file the project commits. Committing it is the point: a clone on a second machine is then the same project rather than a second one that happens to share a name, which is what a shared store needs in order to gather one project's notes from two people.

The file holds the identity, the name it was created under, and when. The identity is what matters; the other two are there so a person reading the file knows what they are looking at.

=>

import { Array, Data, Effect, Match, Option, Schema } from 'effect'
import { existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import { dirname, join } from 'node:path'

export const Identity = Schema.Struct({
  identity: Schema.String,
  name: Schema.String,
  created: Schema.String,
})
export type Identity = typeof Identity.Type

export const identityFile = (directory: string): string =>
  join(directory, '.loom', 'design', 'project.json')

~

Reading is forgiving and writing is not. A project with no file yet, or a file a hand has edited into something unreadable, simply has no identity — that is the ordinary case of a project Design has not seen before, and it answers with `Option.none` rather than a failure. Writing creates the directory it needs and fails loudly if it cannot, since a project whose identity was never written would be created anew on the next run.

=>

export const readIdentity = (
  directory: string,
): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<Identity>> =>
  Effect.sync(() => identityFile(directory)).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((path) =>
      existsSync(path)
        ? Effect.try(() =>
            Schema.decodeUnknownSync(Identity)(JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))),
          ).pipe(Effect.map(Option.some), Effect.catchCause(() => Effect.succeed(Option.none<Identity>())))
        : Effect.succeed(Option.none<Identity>()),
    ),
  )

export const writeIdentity = (
  directory: string,
  identity: Identity,
): Effect.Effect<void, IdentityError> =>
  Effect.try({
    try: () => {
      const path = identityFile(directory)
      mkdirSync(dirname(path), { recursive: true })
      writeFileSync(path, `${JSON.stringify(identity, null, 2)}\n`)
    },
    catch: (cause) => new IdentityError({ directory, cause }),
  })

## Where a run stands

Two facts settle a run: the identity written into the directory, and the row the store keeps under the name. Each may be absent, which makes four combinations, and one of them splits — so five, and every one has an answer that is not a guess.

A project with neither is **new**: Design generates an identity, writes it, and records the row. A project whose file exists but whose row does not is one Design is meeting on a fresh store — a second machine, or a store that was cleared — so it is **adopted** under the identity it already has. A file and a row that agree is the ordinary case, and Design **proceeds**, updating whatever has moved.

The two that disagree are refusals. A name whose row carries a different identity is **taken** — the name belongs to another project, and binding it here would silently re-aim every note already filed under it. A row that exists for a name while the directory holds no identity at all means the command is almost certainly being run somewhere other than the project it names, so it is **misplaced**.

=>

export class IdentityError extends Data.TaggedError('IdentityError')<{
  readonly directory: string
  readonly cause: unknown
}> {}

export type Standing = Data.TaggedEnum<{
  New: { readonly identity: Identity }
  Adopted: { readonly identity: Identity }
  Proceeding: { readonly identity: Identity }
  Taken: { readonly here: Identity; readonly registered: string }
  Misplaced: { readonly registered: string }
}>
export const Standing = Data.taggedEnum<Standing>()

export const standingOf = (
  onDisk: Option.Option<Identity>,
  recorded: Option.Option<string>,
  fresh: () => Identity,
): Standing =>
  Match.value({ onDisk, recorded }).pipe(
    Match.when({ onDisk: Option.isNone, recorded: Option.isNone }, () =>
      Standing.New({ identity: fresh() }),
    ),
    Match.when({ onDisk: Option.isSome, recorded: Option.isNone }, ({ onDisk }) =>
      Standing.Adopted({ identity: onDisk.value }),
    ),
    Match.when({ onDisk: Option.isNone, recorded: Option.isSome }, ({ recorded }) =>
      Standing.Misplaced({ registered: recorded.value }),
    ),
    Match.orElse(({ onDisk, recorded }) => {
      const here = Option.getOrThrow(onDisk)
      const there = Option.getOrThrow(recorded)
      return here.identity === there
        ? Standing.Proceeding({ identity: here })
        : Standing.Taken({ here, registered: there })
    }),
  )

## Which server is actually on that port

The identity file settles whether the command is standing in the right directory. It says nothing about whether the address is serving that project, and a developer with three applications and one habitual port will eventually point Design at the wrong one of them.

For an address on this machine that is answerable. The process listening on a port has a working directory, and a dev server's working directory is its project. Design asks the operating system which process holds the port and where it is running from, and a directory outside the project is a server belonging to something else.

The answer is a hint rather than a verdict. A port on another host, a container, a process owned by another user, or a system without `lsof` all yield nothing, and nothing means the check simply had no opinion — never that the server is wrong.

=>

import { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process'

const LOOPBACK = /^https?:\/\/(?:localhost|127\.0\.0\.1|\[::1\]|0\.0\.0\.0):(\d+)/i

const loopbackPort = (address: string): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(LOOPBACK.exec(address)), (found) => found[1])

const asked = (
  args: ReadonlyArray<string>,
  keep: (line: string) => Option.Option<string>,
): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<string>> =>
  Effect.try(() =>
    execFileSync('lsof', [...args], {
      encoding: 'utf8',
      stdio: ['ignore', 'pipe', 'ignore'],
    }),
  ).pipe(
    Effect.map((output) =>
      Array.head(Array.getSomes(Array.map(output.split('\n'), keep))),
    ),
    Effect.catchCause(() => Effect.succeed(Option.none<string>())),
  )

const listenerOn = (port: string): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<string>> =>
  asked(['-nP', `-iTCP:${port}`, '-sTCP:LISTEN', '-t'], (line) =>
    line.trim() === '' ? Option.none() : Option.some(line.trim()),
  )

const workingDirectoryOf = (pid: string): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<string>> =>
  asked(['-a', '-p', pid, '-d', 'cwd', '-Fn'], (line) =>
    line.startsWith('n') ? Option.some(line.slice(1)) : Option.none(),
  )

export const servedFrom = (
  address: string,
): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<string>> =>
  Option.match(loopbackPort(address), {
    onNone: () => Effect.succeed(Option.none<string>()),
    onSome: (port) =>
      Effect.flatMap(
        listenerOn(port),
        Option.match({
          onNone: () => Effect.succeed(Option.none<string>()),
          onSome: workingDirectoryOf,
        }),
      ),
  })

## Settling it, once, before anything serves

`settle` is the whole question asked and answered: read the file, read the row, decide where the run stands, and write down whatever the decision allows. A run that is new writes the identity file before recording the row, so a project is never recorded under an identity its own directory does not carry. A run that is adopted or proceeding records the row alone, which is what updates a directory or an address that has moved. A run that is refused writes nothing at all.

It answers with the standing rather than acting on it. Refusing is a message to a person, and the wording of that message belongs to the command a person typed, not to the module that reached the verdict.

=>

import { Layer } from 'effect'
import { NoteStore } from './store'

const fresh = (name: string): Identity => ({
  identity: crypto.randomUUID(),
  name,
  created: new Date().toISOString(),
})

export const settle = (options: {
  readonly project: string
  readonly directory: string
  readonly address: string
  readonly store: Layer.Layer<NoteStore>
}): Effect.Effect<Standing, IdentityError> =>
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const store = yield* NoteStore
    const onDisk = yield* readIdentity(options.directory)
    const row = yield* Effect.orDie(store.project(options.project))
    const recorded = Option.flatMap(row, (found) =>
      Option.fromNullishOr(found.identity),
    )
    const standing = standingOf(onDisk, recorded, () => fresh(options.project))
    const record = (identity: Identity) =>
      Effect.orDie(
        store.register(
          options.project,
          identity.identity,
          options.directory,
          options.address,
        ),
      )
    yield* Match.value(standing).pipe(
      Match.tag('New', ({ identity }) =>
        writeIdentity(options.directory, identity).pipe(
          Effect.andThen(record(identity)),
        ),
      ),
      Match.tag('Adopted', ({ identity }) => record(identity)),
      Match.tag('Proceeding', ({ identity }) => record(identity)),
      Match.tag('Taken', () => Effect.void),
      Match.tag('Misplaced', () => Effect.void),
      Match.exhaustive,
    )
    return standing
  }).pipe(Effect.provide(options.store))

